{"type": "message", "id": "2ec2d795", "parentId": "a7f27ae6", "timestamp": "2026-04-16T16:17:11.283Z", "message": {"role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Excellent! I now have the data I need. From the Tesla financial summary PDF (ir.tesla.com/_flysystem/s3/sec/000162828024043432/tsla-20241023-gen.pdf), I found:\n\nQ3-2023: Total revenues 23,350 million ($23.35 billion)\nQ4-2023: Total revenues 25,167 million ($25.17 billion)\n\nAnd from earlier searches:\nQ1 2023: Total revenues ~$23.33 billion, net income $2,539 million\nQ2 2023: Total revenues ~$24.93 billion, net income $2,614 million\nQ3 2023: Total revenues ~$23.35 billion, net income $1,878 million\nQ4 2023: Total revenues ~$25.17 billion, net income $7,943 million\n\nNow I need to calculate the net profit margin percentage for each quarter:\nFormula: (net income / total revenues) × 100\n\nQ1 2023: (2,539 / 23,329) × 100 = 10.89%\nQ2 2023: (2,614 / 24,927) × 100 = 10.45%\nQ3 2023: (1,878 / 23,350) × 100 = 8.03%\nQ4 2023: (7,943 / 25,167) × 100 = 31.55%\n\nWait, I need to verify these numbers more carefully. Let me use the exact numbers from the SEC filings:\n\nFrom the SEC filing (tsla-ex99_1.htm):\n- Q1 2023 net income: 2,539 million\n- Q2 2023 net income: 2,614 million  \n- Q3 2023 net income: 1,878 million\n- Q4 2023 net income: 7,943 million\n\nFrom the financial summary:\n- Q3 2023 total revenues: 23,350 million\n- Q4 2023 total revenues: 25,167 million\n\nFrom earlier data:\n- Q1 2023 total revenues: 23,329 million (from last10k.com showing: 23,329)\n- Q2 2023 total revenues: 24,927 million (from last10k.com showing: 24,927)\n\nLet me calculate:\n\nQ1 2023: (2,539 / 23,329) × 100 = 10.89% > 10.5% ✓\nQ2 2023: (2,614 / 24,927) × 100 = 10.45% - this is exactly 10.45%, which is NOT over 10.5%\nQ3 2023: (1,878 / 23,350) × 100 = 8.03% < 10.5%\nQ4 2023: (7,943 / 25,167) × 100 = 31.55% > 10.5% ✓\n\nSo Q1 and Q4 have net profit margins over 10.5%.\n\nWait, let me double-check Q2. The calculation: 2,614 / 24,927 = 0.1045 = 10.45%\n\n10.45% is NOT over 10.5%, so Q2 does not qualify.\n\nActually, I need to be more precise. Let me recalculate:\nQ1 2023: 2,539 / 23,329 = 0.10889... = 10.89% (over 10.5%)\nQ2 2023: 2,614 / 24,927 = 0.10449... = 10.45% (NOT over 10.5% - it's 10.45%)\nQ3 2023: 1,878 / 23,350 = 0.08025... = 8.03% (NOT over 10.5%)\nQ4 2023: 7,943 / 25,167 = 0.3154... = 31.54% (over 10.5%)\n\nThe question asks for quarters with net profit margin percentages OVER 10.5 percent.\n\nQ1: 10.89% > 10.5% ✓\nQ2: 10.45% < 10.5% ✗\nQ3: 8.03% < 10.5% ✗\nQ4: 31.54% > 10.5% ✓\n\nSo the answer should be Q1, Q4.\n\nBut wait - I should double-check my data sources. The Q4 net income of $7,943 million seems unusually high compared to other quarters. Let me verify this is the correct quarterly net income and not annual or something else.\n\nActually, looking back at the data from the SEC filing excerpt: \"Net income 2,539 2,614 1,878 7,943 1,144\" - this was for Q1-2023, Q2-2023, Q3-2023, Q4-2023, Q1-2024.\n\nThe Q4 2023 net income of $7,943 million (or $7.943 billion) does seem high. This could be due to a one-time tax benefit or other special item. But for the purpose of calculating net profit margin as requested, this is the reported net income from the SEC filings.\n\nLet me verify the Q4 revenue from another source to make sure. From the search results:\n- Teslarati: \"In the fourth quarter, Tesla posted total revenues of $25.1 billion\"\n- CNBC: \"Total revenue increased 3% from $24.3 billion a year earlier\" (this refers to Q4 2023 compared to Q4 2022)\n- Guardian: \"$25.1bn in revenue\"\n\nThe revenue figure of $25.167 billion (25,167 million) from the official Tesla IR PDF matches these sources.\n\nSo my calculations are:\nQ1 2023: (2,539 / 23,329) × 100 = 10.89% > 10.5%\nQ2 2023: (2,614 / 24,927) × 100 = 10.45% NOT > 10.5%\nQ3 2023: (1,878 / 23,350) × 100 = 8.03% < 10.5%\nQ4 2023: (7,943 / 25,167) × 100 = 31.54% > 10.5%\n\nThe quarters with net pro